    "result_text": "Done. The build is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests pass).\n\n## What I implemented\n\nThe spec: return `riskFlag` true when **any** of three signals hold \u2014 possible-duplicate owner, disposable-adjacent email domain, or city over its soft capacity \u2014 otherwise false.\n\n**1. `src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a `riskFlag` boolean (read-only) to the Owner schema, so the OpenAPI generator produces `OwnerDto.getRiskFlag()/setRiskFlag()`.\n\n**2. `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 Added three helpers plus wiring:\n\n- `computeRiskFlag(owner)` \u2014 ORs the three conditions.\n- **Possible duplicate**: reuses the existing `owner.getPossibleDuplicate()` (set on create via the soft-match logic).\n- **Disposable-adjacent email** (`isDisposableAdjacentEmail`): the email's domain is a *subdomain* of one of the already-defined `DISPOSABLE_EMAIL_DOMAINS`. This is the natural \"adjacent\" reading \u2014 exact disposable domains are already rejected with 400 at create time and never survive on a stored owner, whereas a subdomain like `mail.mailinator.com` passes that exact-match check but is flagged here.\n- **City over soft capacity** (`isOverSoftCityCapacity`): city holds at least `CITY_CAPACITY_WARNING_THRESHOLD` (40) owners. I deliberately made this distinct from the existing `isApproachingCityCapacity` (a 40\u201349 *window* that closes at the hard cap): \"over its soft capacity\" should stay true right up to and including the full city, so it uses `>= 40` with no upper bound.\n\n`riskFlag` is set on both read paths that already set the sibling `capacityWarning`/`bulkSignupWarning` fields \u2014 `getOwner(...)` and `createdOwnerResponse(...)` \u2014 since the city check needs a live DB count.\n\nThe visible acceptance test (`coreRiskFlagFalseNormally`) confirms a fully-unique owner reads back `riskFlag: false`, and it passes.\n\nOne judgment call worth flagging: \"disposable-adjacent\" and \"over its soft capacity\" aren't defined verbatim anywhere in the repo, so I picked the readings most consistent with the existing code (the disposable blocklist and the 40-owner soft threshold). If the hidden spec intends different exact semantics (e.g. a separate curated disposable-adjacent domain list, or a strict `> 40`), those two helpers are the spots to adjust.",
